@@ -0,0 +1,9 @@
+I am writing to apply for the Vice President, Information Technology role at Upstream Bio. Having spent the last seven years building and running IT for a pharmaceutical company through the exact arc your organization is now approaching, pre-launch scale-up, clinical development support, and the governance a public company requires, I think the fit is a close one, and the prospect of doing that work again for verekitug is genuinely appealing.
+
+At SK Life Science I joined in September 2018 with two IT staff and roughly three million dollars of annual technology spend, and left in January 2026 having built the function into a nine-person team plus managed-service partners supporting a billion dollars in annual commercial operations. In between I served as Enterprise Architect, then Vice President of Applications, then Head of IT, effectively CIO. I mention the progression because it means I have actually done the unglamorous early part of this job: choosing the first ERP and CRM platforms, negotiating the first serious vendor contracts, deciding what to build and what to leave alone until the company earns the right to spend more on it. Upstream Bio, with two Phase 2 programs underway and commercial readiness still ahead, is closer to that 2018 starting point than to the billion-dollar endpoint, and I would rather be candid about that than pretend otherwise. What I bring is the judgment of having made that journey once already, including where I got the sequencing wrong the first time.
+
+On the regulated and governance side, I owned IT governance, cybersecurity, data privacy, risk management, and disaster recovery for a publicly traded biopharmaceutical company, with ITGC controls designed to US SOX 404 standards under our parent's K-SOX program and tested annually by PwC. I also owned Veeva CTMS and Veeva eTMF for active clinical development, so the clinical systems side of your remit is not new territory. Where the posting asks for practical AI-enabled solutions rather than AI for its own sake, I can point to specific production work: an agent-based security operations workflow that removed a meaningful chunk of manual Level 1 analyst effort, and a Medical Affairs next-best-action model built on our Snowflake platform, both of which kept a human in the loop before anything acted on the output. That distinction, useful automation with the audit trail intact, matters more in a GxP environment than in most.
+
+I also reduced IT operating expenses by 30 percent through vendor renegotiation, cloud rightsizing, and license consolidation, while running a nine-figure vendor portfolio and never missing an SLA. I raise that not to suggest Upstream Bio needs the same cost program, but because a company your size needs someone who treats every dollar of technology spend as a decision worth making deliberately, and I have spent a long time doing exactly that in a regulated setting. I am based in New Jersey and am willing to relocate to the Boston area, with the ability to be onsite within roughly thirty days of an offer.
+
+I would welcome the chance to talk through how the technology foundation I built at SK Life Science might translate to Upstream Bio's next several years, and I am happy to go into more detail on any part of it. Thank you for considering my application.